  "text": "To solve this problem, we need to simplify the given product of logarithmic terms. Let's break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, recall the change of base formula for logarithms:\n\\[\n\\log_a b = \\frac{\\log_c b}{\\log_c a}\n\\]\nwhere \\(c\\) is any positive number.\n\nFor our problem, we have:\n\\[\n\\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{\\log_k(5^{k^2-1})}{\\log_{k+1}(5^{k^2-4})}\n\\]\n\nUsing the change of base formula, we can rewrite each term in the product as follows:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\log_k(5^{k^2-1})}{\\log_{k+1}(5^{k^2-4})} = \\frac{\\frac{\\log_5(5^{k^2-1})}{\\log_5 k}}{\\frac{\\log_5(5^{k^2-4})}{\\log_5 (k+1)}} = \\frac{\\log_5(5^{k^2-1}) \\cdot \\log_5 (k+1)}{\\log_5(5^{k^2-4}) \\cdot \\log_5 k}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying further using the property of logarithms \\(\\log_a a^b = b\\), we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{(k^2-1) \\log_5 (k+1)}{(k^2-4) \\log_5 k}\n\\]\n\nNow, let's write out the product:\n\\[\n\\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{(k^2-1) \\log_5 (k+1)}{(k^2-4) \\log_5 k}\n\\]\n\nNotice that \\(k^2 - 1 = (k-1)(k+1)\\) and \\(k^2 - 4 = (k-2)(k+2)\\). So, we can rewrite the product as:\n\\[\n\\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{(k-1)(k+1) \\log_5 (k+1)}{(k-2)(k+2) \\log_5 k}\n\\]\n\nThis can be separated into three products:\n\\[\n\\left( \\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{k-1}{k-2} \\right) \\left( \\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{k+1}{k+2} \\right) \\left( \\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{\\log_5 (k+1)}{\\log_5 k} \\right)\n\\]\n\nLet's simplify each product separately. For the first product:\n\\[\n\\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{k-1}{k-2} = \\frac{3}{2} \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\cdot \\frac{5}{4} \\cdots \\frac{62}{61}\n\\]\nThis telescopes to:\n\\[\n\\frac{62}{2} = 31\n\\]\n\nFor the second product:\n\\[\n\\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{k+1}{k+2} = \\frac{5}{6} \\cdot \\frac{6}{7} \\cdot \\frac{7}{8} \\cdots \\frac{64}{65}\n\\]\nThis telescopes to:\n\\[\n\\frac{5}{64}\n\\]\n\nFor the third product:\n\\[\n\\prod_{k=4}^{63} \\frac{\\log_5 (k+1)}{\\log_5 k}\n\\]\nThis is simply a telescoping product of ratios of logarithms, which simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\log_5 64}{\\log_5 4} = \\frac{\\log_5 (2^6)}{\\log_5 (2^2)} = \\frac{6 \\log_5 2}{2 \\log_5 2} = 3\n\\]\n\nPutting it all together, we get:\n\\[\n31 \\cdot \\frac{5}{64} \\cdot 3 = \\frac{465}{64}\n\\]\n\nThus, the product is \\(\\frac{465}{64}\\), and \\(m = 465\\) and \\(n = 64\\). Since 465 and 64 are relatively prime, we have \\(m + n = 465 + 64 = 529\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{529}\n\\]",
